---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import StructureFacts from "../../components/StructureFacts.astro";
import SetStructureDemo from "../../components/SetStructureDemo.astro";
import TermCallout from "../../components/TermCallout.astro";
import "../../styles/set-lesson.css";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Observed-remove set"
  description="Alice removes an old beacon while Bob concurrently adds its replacement under a fresh identity."
  territory="Structures"
  sheetTitle="Observed-remove set"
>
  <article class="set-lesson">
    <header class="page-intro">
      <p class="territory-label">Structures</p>
      <h1>Observed-remove set</h1>
      <p class="structure-summary">
        Alice must remove the old Eagle Creek beacon while Bob reports its
        replacement. Carol may receive their records in either order. The
        ranger needs the replacement to remain without restoring the old
        installation.
      </p>
    </header>

    <StructureFacts
      family="CRDT"
      model="State-based observed-remove set"
      updates="Add, remove, and re-add"
      merge="Union addition tags, then subtract observed removals."
      delivery="Safe under repeated and out-of-order delivery"
      bestFor="Collaborative membership where values can return"
      cost="Live addition tags plus retained removal knowledge"
    />

    <section aria-labelledby="identity-title">
      <h2 id="identity-title">A replacement needs a new identity</h2>
      <p>
        Alice's original report gave the old beacon tag <code>A:1</code>.
        After she observes that installation, her removal names
        <code>A:1</code>. It does not remove every future beacon called Eagle
        Creek.
      </p>
      <p>
        Bob adds the replacement during the partition. His addition gets the
        fresh tag <code>B:2</code>. An{" "}
        <dfn><a href="/glossary/#observed-remove-set">observed-remove set</a></dfn>
        {" "}removes only the addition identities that the removing replica
        observed.
      </p>
      <TermCallout
        term="observed-remove set"
        definition="A replicated set whose remove operation targets only addition tags observed by the removing replica."
      />
      <figure>
        <table aria-label="Eagle Creek observed-remove set outcome">
          <thead>
            <tr><th scope="col">Record</th><th scope="col">Meaning</th><th scope="col">Final state</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>A:1</code></th><td>Old installation</td><td>Removed</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>B:2</code></th><td>Replacement</td><td>Live</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <figcaption>Carol can receive the records in either order and still retain only the replacement.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </section>

    <SetStructureDemo
      kind="or-set"
      title="Keep the replacement, not the old beacon"
      description="Alice removes the installation she observed while Bob concurrently installs a replacement under a fresh identity."
      rule="Remove observed tags; keep concurrent fresh tags."
      raceLabel="Race removal against replacement"
    />

    <section aria-labelledby="add-wins-title">
      <h2 id="add-wins-title">The concurrent replacement wins</h2>
      <p>
        Alice could not name <code>B:2</code> when she removed the old beacon
        because Bob had not sent it yet. Bob's addition is concurrent with her
        removal, so it survives. This is the set's add-wins rule.
      </p>
      <p>
        A delayed copy of <code>A:1</code> does not return. The replicas retain
        removal knowledge for that exact tag. The visible value is still Eagle
        Creek, but its surviving identity proves that it is the replacement.
      </p>
      <p>
        The full reference lesson lets you partition Alice and Bob, preserve a
        stale copy, exchange the conflicting deltas, and inspect the live tags
        and tombstones.
      </p>
      <p class="lesson-entry">
        <a href="/atlas/observed-remove-sets/">Open the interactive observed-remove set lesson</a>
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="notes-title">
      <h2 id="notes-title">Sources and implementation</h2>
      <p>
        The interactive lesson calls Watershed's{" "}
        <a href="https://github.com/tylerbutler/watershed/blob/4a8739323ee491f353fcaa8ccfb0488419c1cd43/src/watershed/or_set_kernel.gleam">OR-set kernel</a>
        {" "}through the stable Atlas toolkit.
      </p>
    </section>

    <nav class="structure-next" aria-label="Continue through set structures">
      <a href="/structures/two-p-set/">Back to TwoPSet</a>
      <a href="/structures/sets/">Browse set family</a>
      <a href="/structures/registers/">Continue to registers</a>
    </nav>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .lesson-entry {
    margin-top: var(--space-5);
    font-family: var(--font-ui);
    font-weight: 700;
  }
</style>
